  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at height 0.4 x loss with 8 contours. x is the steep axis. Without noise, a step multiplies x by 1 - 2.5 x rate, so swings start above rate 0.4 and grow above rate 0.8.",
    "Each slope component gets Gaussian noise with spread 1.5 x sqrt((64 - B) / (63 B)), like sampling B of 64 points without replacement. It is 1.5 at B = 1 and exactly zero at B = 64. A noisy step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The purple arrow is the mini-batch step (minus rate x noisy slope), the way the ball moves next. The dark arrow is the full-data step (minus rate x true slope). Both are drawn 2.5 times their true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 6. The ball is drawn on the bowl at the rim, and the readout shows the true loss.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence. New run, keep last path carries on with the next values. Reset replays the sequence from the start and clears both paths."
  ]
}